# All-root finite ranked-H1 Hunter closure

Status: `FINITE-EXACT + VERIFIED` after completion of the recorded
ordinary/optimized shard parity.  Scope is the `5,999` rank-four/H1
branches with exact discrepancy cutoff `N<=15000`; the `181` larger-cutoff
branches are outside this route.

## Result

```text
roots                                  3432
marked gate branches                  41415
scalar-hard branches                  14806
rank-four H1 eligible                  6180
finite N<=15000 branches               5999
ordered-star closures                  5975
nonstar Hunter-tree closures             24
witnesses                                 0
deferred cutoff branches                181
whole roots closed                       132
new beyond official THM-2895/2898/
  2899/2901/2902 union                   121
official proved union                    138
official residual                       3294
```

The `24` star survivors contain exactly `54` star-hostile five-sets.  Exact
maximum spanning trees repair all `54`; the smallest strict Hunter margin
is `1799771/75716368`.  The finite cores have size quantiles

```text
min 5, p25 8, median 16, p75 36, p90 83, p95 141,
p99 283, max 444.
```

The raw largest five-set universe is

```text
binom(444,5)=140,578,526,088.
```

## Proof architecture

The main verifier imports only the tracked THM-2899 ranked-suffix engine,
which in turn hash-pins the gate and interval engines.  For every branch it
reconstructs the actual literal apex residual, actual excluded gate
prefix, strict rank-four margin, exact discrepancy cutoff, and complete
`H1` core.  It checks the ordered later-core star certificate and falls
back to an exact residual search only as an independent control.

The Hunter audit reopens precisely the `24` star survivors.  It enumerates
only star-hostile five-sets, computes every required pair intersection from
literal residual subtraction, runs exact maximum-spanning-tree Kruskal,
and checks `Psi5<h`.

The merge verifier hash-pins all 32 deterministic shard outputs, checks
that the body atlas is covered once, merges exact counts and core
histograms, checks the one-index-zero-pivot anatomy, and locks the complete
`132`-root list and its difference from the official `17`.

The merge verifier does **not** recompute the interval mathematics inside
the shards.  Therefore, in the present architecture, the 32 optimized raw
shard outputs are canonical proof artifacts.  The byte-identical ordinary
copies are replay controls and need not both be promoted.  Alternatively,
a future orchestrator may replay all shards into a temporary directory and
then invoke the same merge checks, in which case the raw files can become
regenerable rather than canonical.

